<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
<%- include("partials/head", { title: "Methodology - Irish language map", mapPage: false }) %>
</head>

<body>
<%- include("partials/nav", { active: "methodology" }) %>

    <div class="page">
        <header class="masthead">
            <img class="masthead-spot" src="img/spot-decline.svg" alt="">
            <h1>Methodology</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                What the figures measure, where they come from, and how carefully
                to compare them across the years.
            </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prose">
            <%# The three things to keep in mind, before the detail. %>
            <aside class="note-card">
                <span class="note-tag">In short</span>
                <ul class="hand-list">
                    <li>Every figure is people <strong>saying</strong> they can speak Irish, not a test, and not how often they use it.</li>
                    <li>The census questions changed over time, so read the long trend as a guide, not a precise measurement.</li>
                    <li>Northern Ireland has no county figures from 1926 to 1986, and some of its figures are estimates.</li>
                </ul>
            </aside>

            <h2>What is measured</h2>
            <p>
                Each figure is the percentage of people aged 3 and over who
                reported, in that census, that they could speak Irish. It is
                self-reported ability, not a test of fluency and not a measure of
                how often the language is used.
            </p>

            <h2>Comparing across years</h2>
            <p>
                Census questions and definitions changed over time, so
                comparisons between distant years should be interpreted
                cautiously. The CSO notes that results such as 1926 and 2022 are
                not directly comparable, and a new question series began in 1996.
            </p>
            <p>
                That is worth keeping in mind for the jump from 1991 to 1996 in
                the story: part of a rise that fast can come from how the question
                was asked or answered.
            </p>

            <h2>Northern Ireland</h2>
            <p>
                Northern Ireland recorded Irish only in the all-island censuses
                of 1851 to 1911 and again from 1991, so the six counties show as
                &ldquo;no data&rdquo; in between. Its 2001 and 2021 censuses appear
                under 2002 and 2022 to line up with the Republic; the county panel
                says so when that applies. Antrim and Down for 1851, 1861 and 1901
                are estimated, because Belfast straddles both counties and was not
                split between them in those returns.
            </p>
            <p>
                Some Northern Ireland county figures are themselves estimates.
                The 1991, 2001 and 2011 values were derived from ward-level
                returns, and NISRA&rsquo;s 2021 county figures are estimated from
                census areas, as the North does not publish this on a county
                basis directly.
            </p>

            <h2>The history in the story</h2>
            <p>
                The notes beside the map and the chart give historical context.
                They are not findings of this project, and the census alone cannot
                show what caused a change. They draw on:
            </p>
            <ul class="hand-list">
                <li>On the Famine and the language: <a href="https://encyclopedia.com/international/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/language-and-literacy-decline-irish-language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Language and Literacy: Decline of the Irish Language</a>, and <a href="https://www.irishfamine.ie/?p=14" target="_blank" rel="noopener">An Gorta M&oacute;r</a>.</li>
                <li>On the national schools of 1831, and the debate over their part in the decline: <a href="https://heritage.wicklowheritage.org/?p=19996" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Irish Language in Co. Wicklow</a>.</li>
                <li>On Irish as a compulsory subject from 1922: <a href="https://historyireland.com/?p=34779" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Irish made compulsory in schools</a> (History Ireland).</li>
                <li>On the Northern Ireland census dropping and restoring the question: <a href="https://blogs.qub.ac.uk/qpol/?p=9965" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A century of change in Northern Ireland, 1926&ndash;2026</a> (Queen&rsquo;s University Belfast).</li>
                <li>On the new question in 1996, and on why 1926 and 2022 are not directly comparable: the CSO&rsquo;s <a href="https://data.gov.ie/en_GB/dataset/b1103-2002-percentage-of-irish-speakers-aged-3-years-and-over-new-question-in-1996/resource/6b424f94-99fe-494a-89f2-7e53c189f28f" target="_blank" rel="noopener">series notes</a> and <a href="https://www.cso.ie/en/releasesandpublications/ep/p-tnli/thenandnowlifeinirelandin1926and2022/keyfindings/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1926 and 2022 comparison</a>.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>The drawings</h2>
            <p>
                The maps and charts are drawn to look sketched by hand. The county
                outlines are simplified and given a slight wobble: easy to
                recognise, but not a survey-accurate map. The numbers behind them
                are unchanged.
            </p>

            <h2>Sources</h2>
            <ul class="hand-list">
                <li>Republic of Ireland: Central Statistics Office (<a href="https://data.cso.ie/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data.cso.ie</a>).</li>
                <li>Northern Ireland: the Northern Ireland censuses, compiled by <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/phelimbirch.bsky.social" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phelim Birch</a>.</li>
                <li>Boundaries: Ordnance Survey Ireland and Ordnance Survey of Northern Ireland.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

<%- include("partials/footer") %>
    </div>

<script src="js/nav.js?v=<%= assetVersion %>"></script>

</body>

</html>
